.TH PERMISSIONS 7 "2025-09-14" "Local Manual" "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
permissions \- Linux file permission cheat sheet
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every directory entry has an owner, a group, and a set of permission bits.
The familiar mode string shown by
.BR ls (1)
has this shape:
.PP
.EX
  File Type     User (Owner)         Group            Others (World)
   [ - ]          [ rwx ]           [ r-x ]              [ r-- ]

     │            ╱  │  ╲           ╱  │  ╲              ╱  │  ╲
     │           r   w   x         r   -   x            r   -   -
     │
     │           │   │   │         │   │   │            │   │   └── No Exec
     │           │   │   │         │   │   └── Execute  │   └────── No Write
     │           │   │   │         │   └────── No Write └────────── Read
     │           │   │   │         └────────── Read
     │           │   │   └──── Execute
     │           │   └──────── Write
     │           └──────────── Read
     │
     └───>  - = Regular File      d = Directory      l = Symbolic Link
            c = Character Device  b = Block Device   p = Named Pipe
            s = Socket
.EE
.PP
For example, the mode string
\fB\-rwxr\-xr\-\-\fR means a regular file whose owner may read, write, and execute it; whose
group may read and execute it; and whose other users may only read it.
.SH PERMISSION BITS
.TS
box tab(|);
l l l l.
Bit|Value|File|Directory
_
r (read)|4|Read contents|List names
w (write)|2|Change contents|Create/remove/rename
x (execute)|1|Run file|Traverse/search
- (absent)|0|Denied|Denied
.TE
.PP
Directory permissions combine: to access a file by pathname, the user
normally needs
.B x
on every parent directory.  To remove or rename an entry, the relevant
permission is on its parent directory, not on the entry itself.  A file's
.B x
bit does not make it executable if its contents are not a valid program or
script.
.SH NUMERIC MODES
Each three-digit octal number describes owner, group, and others.  Add the
values of the desired bits:
.PP
.EX
      r = 4       w = 2       x = 1

      7 = rwx      6 = rw-      5 = r-x      4 = r--
      3 = -wx      2 = -w-      1 = --x      0 = ---

      chmod 754 file        # rwxr-xr--
            7     5  4
          owner group other
.EE
.PP
Common modes:
.TS
box tab(|);
l l l.
Mode|Symbolic|Typical use
_
600|rw-------|Private file
640|rw-r-----|File readable by its group
644|rw-r--r--|Ordinary non-executable file
700|rwx------|Private directory or program
750|rwxr-x---|Directory or program shared with a group
755|rwxr-xr-x|Publicly traversable directory or executable
.TE
.PP
The mode is not the whole access decision.  The effective user, group
membership, ACLs, mount options, Linux security modules, and the filesystem
can impose additional restrictions.
.SH SYMBOLIC MODES
.BR chmod (1)
accepts symbolic changes.  The who part is
.BR u
(owner),
.BR g
(group),
.BR o
(others), or
.BR a
(all).  Operators are
.B +
(add),
.B -
(remove), and
.B =
(set exactly):
.PP
.EX
  chmod u+x script       # add execute for owner
  chmod g-w file         # remove write from group
  chmod o= file          # remove all permissions from others
  chmod a+r file         # add read for everyone
  chmod u=rw,go=r file   # set rw-r--r--
  chmod -R go-rwx dir    # recursively remove group/other access
.EE
.PP
Use care with
.BR chmod (1)\ -R:
its result may be inappropriate when files and directories need different
modes.
.SH SPECIAL BITS
The optional fourth octal digit (or the leading bit in a four-digit mode)
sets special behavior:
.PP
.EX
       4     set-user-ID (setuid)
       2     set-group-ID (setgid)
       1     sticky bit

       chmod 4755 program    # rwsr-xr-x
       chmod 2755 program    # rwxr-sr-x
       chmod 1777 directory  # rwxrwxrwt
.EE
.TP
.B setuid (4000)
For an executable file, run with the file owner's effective UID.  Linux may
ignore setuid on some filesystems, mounts, or execution contexts.  It is
usually not meaningful on directories.
.TP
.B setgid (2000)
For an executable file, run with the file group's effective GID.  On a
directory, newly created entries inherit the directory's group, and new
subdirectories inherit the setgid bit on Linux filesystems that support it.
.TP
.B sticky (1000)
On a directory, users may remove or rename entries only when they own the
entry, own the directory, or have appropriate privilege.  The usual example
is
.BR /tmp .
The sticky bit is displayed as
.B t
in place of the others' execute bit (or
.B T
when execute is absent).
.PP
When a special bit replaces an execute bit, lower-case
.B s
or
.B t
means execute is also set; upper-case
.B S
or
.B T
means it is not.
.SH DEFAULT PERMISSIONS AND UMASK
Programs commonly start with a requested mode and the process umask removes
permissions:
.PP
.EX
  effective mode = requested mode & ~umask

  umask 022
  touch file       # commonly 644 (0666 & ~0022)
  mkdir directory  # commonly 755 (0777 & ~0022)
.EE
.PP
A umask does not grant permissions; it only masks bits requested by a
program.  View or set it with
.BR umask (1)
(or the shell's built-in command).  Exact defaults can vary by program,
ACLs, and the user's environment.
.SH INSPECTING ACCESS
.EX
  ls -l file                 # mode, owner, and group
  stat file                  # decimal/octal details and timestamps
  namei -l /path/to/file     # permissions on every path component
  getfacl file               # POSIX ACLs, if present
  id                         # effective identity and groups
  test -r file && echo yes   # test access as the current process
.EE
.PP
For a symbolic link,
.BR ls (1)
normally displays the link's own mode as
.B lrwxrwxrwx;
access checks follow the link and use the target's permissions.  Use
.BR stat (1)\ -L
or
.BR readlink (1)
to examine the target or link as needed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chmod (1),
.BR chown (1),
.BR chgrp (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR stat (1),
.BR umask (1),
.BR acl (5),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR inode (7),
.BR path_resolution (7)
.SH NOTES
This page is a quick reference, not a complete security model.  In
particular, root privileges, capabilities, ACLs, and security modules can
change the result of a simple mode-bit analysis.
